course: dictionary
lesson: 14044
title: Who makes it?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A manufacturer is a person or company that makes goods in large numbers, usually in a factory using machines. Look for the word on boxes, toys and kettles. «The toy manufacturer checked every doll before it went into the shop.» The manufacturer made the dolls, so it is the maker, not the shop that sells them.
explain: The word has a second meaning: the company named as the maker of a product. «If the lamp stops working, contact the manufacturer.» Here the manufacturer is the company whose name is printed on the lamp.
explain: Look for clues in the words around manufacturer. Car manufacturer tells you it makes cars. Manufacturer's instructions tells you the instructions come from the company that made the product. «Always follow the manufacturer's instructions when you use a new kettle.» Try swapping in maker: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Manufacturer comes from words meaning "make by hand" or "make", though today most goods are made by machines.
fact: A manufacturer usually makes goods in large numbers, not just one at a time.
fact: «Car manufacturers» build cars in big factories.
fact: Manufacturers turn raw materials, such as wood, metal or cotton, into products that people can buy.
fact: «Contact the manufacturer» means ask the company that made the product for help.
exercise: What does manufacturer mean? | A teacher who explains how machines work | A person who mends old clothes | *A company that makes goods in large numbers | A shop that sells toys to children
exercise: «Follow the manufacturer's instructions when you plug in the kettle.» Whose instructions are these? | The child who is using it | The person who sold the kettle | The electrician next door | *The company that made the kettle
exercise: «Car manufacturers use robots to build cars in big factories.» What do car manufacturers do? | *Make cars in large numbers | Sell second-hand cars | Paint road signs | Drive cars to customers
test: Manufacturers turn raw materials into what? | Holidays | *Products | Weather | Lessons
test: «The toy broke, so Sam wrote to the manufacturer.» Who did Sam write to? | *The company that made the toy | His teacher | The shop that sold the toy | The postman
exam: If your new toy breaks, what should you do? | Wait for it to fix itself | Ask your friend to fix it | *Contact the manufacturer whose name is on the box | Throw it away at once
